    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d622">

                <pb facs="0682" n="674" sameAs="frus:frus1939v02/pg674"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">125.981/57: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Warsaw</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Davis</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi><anchor n="10" xml:id="d622-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Warsaw</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-09-11">September 11, 1939</date>.<lb/>[Received September
                        16—10:07 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4. No reply received to Consulate General’s telegrams of past week. Although
                    Warsaw bombarded no casualties as yet among officers and 80 persons who have
                    taken refuge in Embassy including 50 women and children. Consulate General
                    remaining in Embassy whose protective facilities are poor but no better refuge
                    available or reachable. Please inform Bark.<anchor n="11" xml:id="d622-f2"/>
                    Also inform missions where officers’ wives residing that husbands are well.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Davis</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d622-f1" n="10" type="source" xml:id="d622-f1-content">
                <p>The text of this telegram was sent by the Department on September 16 to the
                    Ambassador in Germany (No. 522), to the Minister in Rumania (No. 122), and to
                    the Minister in Norway (No. 38) with instructions to repeat to the Minister In
                    Denmark.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d622-f2" n="11" xml:id="d622-f2-content">
                <p>Alexander C. Kirk, American Chargé in Germany.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
